#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Give agents' commits a repo's slow checks, through git's own pre-commit hook.

A repo keeps its fast checks in the ordinary pre-commit hook, which every
committer runs. Checks too slow for that (the full test suite, say) go in an
executable named `agent-pre-commit` beside it, in whatever directory git takes
hooks from (`core.hooksPath`, else .git/hooks).

A fix commit (fixup!, squash!, amend!) that is small, at most 20 lines changed in
files it only modifies in place, none binary and none a test, runs the optional
`agent-quick-check`
beside it instead, the repo's fast static checks, or nothing when the repo has
none: wt-ship runs the full check on each commit it lands that has no pass on the
record below. Any other fix commit runs the full check, or the commit check where
the repo keeps one. The gate knows a fix commit by --fixup or --squash, or by its
subject, given with -m or in the file -F names; not with `-F -`, since git reads
stdin only after the hook has run.

The commit check: a repo that keeps an executable `agent-commit-check` beside
`agent-pre-commit` runs it at each commit that isn't a small fix, in the full
check's place, with its passes on a record of their own,
`agent-commit-check-passed`, beside the full one. wt-ship reads only the full
record, so it runs `agent-pre-commit` on each such commit when it lands. The line
--install writes says its run is a commit's by setting AGENT_CHECK_AT_COMMIT=1; the
gate takes it out of the environment of every check, and a run without it, such as
wt-ship's recheck or a hook still on an older line, runs the full check. At a
commit, CHECK_BASE and the skip below count a pass of either check; otherwise only
the full check's. The commit check waits its turn in heavy's queue unless it names
$CHECK_QUEUE_EXIT, as below.

`--install REPO...` puts one line first in each repo's pre-commit hook (creating
the hook if needed). When `$AI_AGENT` is set, as Claude Code sets it in its
shells, that line exports AGENT_PRE_COMMIT=1 and runs the whole hook again, which
skips the line; once that passes, it runs agent-pre-commit and fails the commit
if it fails. So the hook's own checks run first, in whatever language, and what
its formatters rewrite and stage is in the index before the agent check starts:
the check sees what is committed, and the tree it records is the commit's. Git
runs the hook after `commit -a` or `commit PATHS` has updated the index, so this
holds for those too. The rerun's parent is the hook's shell, not git, and it sees
AGENT_PRE_COMMIT, which also stops the loop when agent-pre-commit runs pre-commit
itself. Whatever ran above the line would run twice, so --install moves a line
with a command above it to the top (comments above it may stay), and replaces an
older line, wherever it is, with this one at the top. The line runs the check
through `--run`; an agent without agent-commit-gate on PATH, such as a
teammate's, runs the check directly instead, every time. A check `--run` runs
waits its turn in heavy's queue (bin/heavy), so agents' commit checks never run
at once, and prints heavy's waiting lines; one that needs no build or test can
skip the queue, as below.
wt-ship rechecks through `--run` too, first with `--no-queue`: a check that would wait
in heavy's queue then doesn't run there, and --run exits 75, recording nothing, for
wt-ship to run it in one job with the landing's other such checks. While wt-ship checks a
worktree's commits before its tip, each checked out there in turn, a marker in the
worktree's git dir names the run, and `--run` refuses a commit in that worktree, which
would be lost when the tip comes back. The checks wt-ship starts carry its PID in
CHECKING_EARLIER_COMMITS_PID, and run (`wt-ship -h`).

No check `--run` runs gets a variable whose name marks a secret: one of the name's
`_`-separated parts, in any case, is KEY, TOKEN, SECRET, PASSWORD or PASS, as in
FILEDRAWER_STAGING_KEY or GH_TOKEN (MONKEY and SSH_AUTH_SOCK stay). Nor does it get
BASH_ENV, the file every non-interactive bash sources first: in the sandbox it sources
the secrets file, which would give them back to each bash the check starts; what else
it sets is in the environment already. That holds for each check, at a commit or in
wt-ship's recheck, in every repo. A test's failure message once printed the
environment a subprocess received, and a secret's value reached an agent's output. A
job that needs a secret runs as `heavy -- CMD`, which keeps the environment. This
catches a mistake, not a check set on a secret: one can still read the secrets file,
or the environment of the process that ran it in /proc.

`--run` records each tree that passes in agent-pre-commit-passed, in the repo's
common git dir, which every worktree shares: the last 1,000, each with the SHA-1
of agent-pre-commit's bytes, so an edited check reuses no pass of its old self.
A pass counts only with no unstaged changes to tracked files, before the check
and after it, since some checks read the working tree. A check that passes but
leaves what's staged changed refuses the commit: it didn't see what git would
commit. That happens when a check restages a file itself, or when a `git add`
runs while the check waits its turn, since git reads the index again after the
hook. When the tree being checked is HEAD's own and on the record, the check is
skipped: an amend that changes only the message, or a recheck with nothing
rebased. Any other tree runs it, a revert to a tree that passed included, since
a check may read more than the tree.

Before it runs the check, `--run` looks through HEAD's first-parent history,
HEAD and up to 99 commits before it, for the nearest commit whose tree passed
the check (at a commit with a commit check, either check), and exports it as
$CHECK_BASE (a full SHA; unset when none is found). A repo's check may then run
only what the changes since that commit
need. To list the changed paths, use
    git diff --cached --name-only "$CHECK_BASE"
which works at commit time, when the index holds what's staged, and on a
recheck, when the index matches the commit being checked. Add -z for paths git
would quote, --no-renames to list both sides of a rename, and run it before
unsetting git's variables or changing directory: `commit -a` and `commit
PATHS` stage into an index of their own, which GIT_INDEX_FILE names. On
wt-ship's recheck the base is usually the commit before, or for the first
commit it checks, the main branch's tip, which passed when it landed. A check that
ignores
CHECK_BASE runs in full, as does any check when no base is found. The pass line
names the base it was given. agent-quick-check gets CHECK_BASE too: the nearest
commit that passed agent-pre-commit, whose checks include the quick ones.

A check that runs only what the change needs may need no build or test at all, as
for a docs-only commit, and then has no reason to wait behind other sessions' builds.
Such a check says so by naming $CHECK_QUEUE_EXIT anywhere in its file (agent-pre-commit,
agent-commit-check or agent-quick-check). `--run` then runs it first outside heavy's
queue, pinned to one
core with CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=1, and with CHECK_QUEUE_EXIT=75. Before it starts a build or
a test, the check exits with that code, after printing a line saying why; `--run`
shows the line, waits its turn in the queue as for any other check, and runs it again
there without the variable. Any other exit is the check's outcome, and a pass line
then says it ran outside heavy's queue. A check that doesn't name it waits in the
queue (agent-pre-commit, agent-commit-check) or runs outside it (agent-quick-check),
as before, and never sees an inherited CHECK_QUEUE_EXIT. The one core keeps a check
whose handling broke
from loading the machine. Beyond that the queue works on trust, as the --no-verify
refusal below does: a check that names the variable and never exits with its code
never waits.

Without arguments it is a Claude Code PreToolUse hook (install.py registers it
in ~/.claude/settings.json) that refuses `git commit --no-verify` (or `-n`),
which would skip the check. It catches a mistake, not an agent set on getting
around the check (`AI_AGENT= git commit`, `AGENT_PRE_COMMIT=1 git commit`, `git
-c core.hooksPath=...`, or a line written into the pass record by hand, which
every worktree then trusts).

    agent-commit-gate --install REPO...    # add the line to each repo's pre-commit
    agent-commit-gate --run HOOK           # as the line calls it
    agent-commit-gate < hook-input.json    # as Claude Code calls it
"""

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from contextlib import ExitStack, contextmanager, suppress
from pathlib import Path
from typing import NamedTuple

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib"))
import commitcheck
import heavyqueue
import passrecord

HOOK_NAME = "agent-pre-commit"
COMMENT = (
    "# For an agent's commit, the next line reruns this whole hook, then the slower"
    f" {HOOK_NAME} on what it staged; keep it first (llm-toolbox agent-commit-gate)."
)
# The line runs the whole hook again as a program, where it skips itself, so what the
# hook formats and stages is in the index before the check starts. The hook is shared,
# so an agent without llm-toolbox runs the check directly, every time, and its refusal
# still ends with the line --run prints. AT_COMMIT tells --run it runs for a commit,
# where a repo's COMMIT_CHECK_NAME may stand in for HOOK_NAME.
AT_COMMIT = "AGENT_CHECK_AT_COMMIT"
LINE = (
    'if [ -n "${AI_AGENT:-}" ] && [ -z "${AGENT_PRE_COMMIT:-}" ]; then '
    'export AGENT_PRE_COMMIT=1; "$0" || exit; '
    "if command -v agent-commit-gate >/dev/null; then "
    f'{AT_COMMIT}=1 agent-commit-gate --run "$(dirname "$0")/{HOOK_NAME}"; '
    f'else "$(dirname "$0")/{HOOK_NAME}" || '
    f'{{ echo "COMMIT REFUSED: {HOOK_NAME} failed" >&2; exit 1; }}; fi; exit; fi'
)
# Earlier comments above LINE, which --install removes with the line below them.
OLD_COMMENTS = (
    f"# Agents' commits also run the slower {HOOK_NAME} (llm-toolbox agent-commit-gate).",
)
# Earlier versions of LINE, which --install replaces: before --run, before the refusal
# line, before the line ran the hook first, then before it said it runs for a commit.
OLD_LINES = (
    (
        'if [ -n "${AI_AGENT:-}" ] && [ -z "${AGENT_PRE_COMMIT:-}" ]; then '
        f'AGENT_PRE_COMMIT=1 "$(dirname "$0")/{HOOK_NAME}" || exit; fi'
    ),
    (
        'if [ -n "${AI_AGENT:-}" ] && [ -z "${AGENT_PRE_COMMIT:-}" ]; then '
        "if command -v agent-commit-gate >/dev/null; then "
        f'AGENT_PRE_COMMIT=1 agent-commit-gate --run "$(dirname "$0")/{HOOK_NAME}"; '
        f'else AGENT_PRE_COMMIT=1 "$(dirname "$0")/{HOOK_NAME}"; fi || exit; fi'
    ),
    (
        'if [ -n "${AI_AGENT:-}" ] && [ -z "${AGENT_PRE_COMMIT:-}" ]; then '
        "if command -v agent-commit-gate >/dev/null; then "
        f'AGENT_PRE_COMMIT=1 agent-commit-gate --run "$(dirname "$0")/{HOOK_NAME}" || exit; '
        f'else AGENT_PRE_COMMIT=1 "$(dirname "$0")/{HOOK_NAME}" || '
        f'{{ echo "COMMIT REFUSED: {HOOK_NAME} failed" >&2; exit 1; }}; fi; fi'
    ),
    (
        'if [ -n "${AI_AGENT:-}" ] && [ -z "${AGENT_PRE_COMMIT:-}" ]; then '
        'export AGENT_PRE_COMMIT=1; "$0" || exit; '
        "if command -v agent-commit-gate >/dev/null; then "
        f'agent-commit-gate --run "$(dirname "$0")/{HOOK_NAME}"; '
        f'else "$(dirname "$0")/{HOOK_NAME}" || '
        f'{{ echo "COMMIT REFUSED: {HOOK_NAME} failed" >&2; exit 1; }}; fi; exit; fi'
    ),
)
# The parts of a variable's name that mark it a secret, which no check gets.
SECRET_PARTS = frozenset({"KEY", "TOKEN", "SECRET", "PASSWORD", "PASS"})
# How many of HEAD's first-parent commits, HEAD included, --run looks through for one
# whose tree passed the check: the check's CHECK_BASE.
BASE_SEARCH = 100
REDIRECT = re.compile(r"^\d*[<>]")
HEREDOC = re.compile(r"<<-?\s*(['\"]?)([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\1")
ASSIGNMENT = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=")
SHELLS = {"sh", "bash", "dash", "ksh", "zsh"}

# git's own options (before the subcommand) whose value is the next word.
GIT_VALUE = {"-C", "-c", "--git-dir", "--work-tree", "--namespace", "--config-env"}
# git accepts any unambiguous prefix of a long option; shorter ones clash with --no-verbose.
NO_VERIFY = "--no-verify"
NO_VERIFY_SHORTEST = len("--no-veri")
# git commit options whose value is the next word; u and S take only an attached one.
COMMIT_SHORT_VALUE = set("mFCct")
COMMIT_SHORT_ATTACHED = set("uS")
COMMIT_LONG_VALUE = {
    "--message",
    "--file",
    "--author",
    "--date",
    "--reuse-message",
    "--reedit-message",
    "--fixup",
    "--squash",
    "--cleanup",
    "--template",
    "--trailer",
}


def strip_heredocs(command):
    """The command without heredoc bodies: their lines are data, not commands."""
    kept, closing = [], []
    for line in command.split("\n"):
        if closing:
            if line.strip() == closing[0]:
                closing.pop(0)
            continue
        kept.append(line)
        closing += [m.group(2) for m in HEREDOC.finditer(line)]
    return "\n".join(kept)


def segments(command):
    """The simple commands in a shell command: split on &&, ||, ;, | and newlines
    outside quotes, so a quoted message never splits."""
    parts, current, quote, i = [], [], None, 0
    while i < len(command):
        char = command[i]
        if quote:
            current.append(char)
            if char == "\\" and quote == '"' and i + 1 < len(command):
                current.append(command[i + 1])
                i += 1
            elif char == quote:
                quote = None
        elif char in "'\"":
            quote = char
            current.append(char)
        elif command.startswith(("&&", "||"), i):
            parts.append("".join(current))
            current = []
            i += 1
        elif char in ";|\n":
            parts.append("".join(current))
            current = []
        else:
            current.append(char)
        i += 1
    parts.append("".join(current))
    return parts


def git_subcommand(words):
    """(the words after git's own options, the -C directories among those options),
    for a git command line without its leading `git`."""
    rest, directories = list(words), []
    while rest and rest[0].startswith("-"):
        option = rest.pop(0)
        if option in GIT_VALUE and rest:
            value = rest.pop(0)
            if option == "-C":
                directories.append(value)
    return rest, directories


def commits(command):
    """(arguments, directory) for each `git commit` in a shell command: the directory
    it runs in, relative to the command's own, from a `cd DIR` before it and `git -C
    DIR`. Heredoc bodies are skipped; arguments stop at the first redirection."""
    directory = Path()
    for segment in segments(strip_heredocs(command)):
        try:
            words = shlex.split(segment)
        except ValueError:
            words = segment.split()
        while words and ASSIGNMENT.match(words[0]):
            words = words[1:]
        if len(words) == 2 and words[0] == "cd":
            directory = directory / words[1]
            continue
        if not words or Path(words[0]).name != "git":
            continue
        rest, directories = git_subcommand(words[1:])
        here = directory.joinpath(*directories)
        if rest[:1] != ["commit"]:
            continue
        args = []
        for word in rest[1:]:
            if REDIRECT.match(word):
                break
            args.append(word)
        yield args, here


FIX_SUBJECT = ("fixup!", "squash!", "amend!")
# The optional fast check a small fix commit runs instead of HOOK_NAME, beside it.
QUICK_NAME = "agent-quick-check"
# The optional check every other commit runs instead of HOOK_NAME, beside it, when the
# line says the run is a commit's; wt-ship runs HOOK_NAME on the commit when it lands.
COMMIT_CHECK_NAME = "agent-commit-check"
# A check that names this variable runs first outside heavy's queue, with it set to
# QUEUE_EXIT, the exit status that asks for the queue (sysexits' EX_TEMPFAIL).
QUEUE_EXIT_NAME = "CHECK_QUEUE_EXIT"
QUEUE_EXIT = commitcheck.QUEUE_EXIT


def names_queue_exit(content):
    """Whether a check, from its bytes, names QUEUE_EXIT_NAME, and so runs first
    outside heavy's queue."""
    return QUEUE_EXIT_NAME.encode() in content


# A fix commit is small when it changes at most this many lines, only in files it
# modifies, none of them a test.
SMALL_FIX_LINES = 20
TEST_NAME = re.compile(r"(^test_|_test\.|\.test\.|\.spec\.)")


def is_test_path(path):
    """Whether `path` looks like a test: under a test(s) directory, or named so."""
    parts = Path(path).parts
    return any(part in ("test", "tests") for part in parts[:-1]) or bool(
        TEST_NAME.search(parts[-1])
    )


# Modes a small fix may not touch: a submodule's commit and a symlink's target each
# show as a line or two however much they change.
GITLINK, SYMLINK = "160000", "120000"


def is_small_fix():
    """Whether the staged change is a small fix: at most SMALL_FIX_LINES lines changed,
    every file modified in place (none added, deleted, renamed or copied, no mode
    changed, no submodule or symlink), none binary and none a test. Such a fix is
    likely a lint or syntax repair, which the full check can wait for until
    wt-ship lands it. An empty change, as in a reword, is small. -z keeps paths as
    they are: without it git quotes one with a tab, a quote or a non-ASCII letter."""
    raw = git_out("diff", "--cached", "--raw", "-z", "-M", "HEAD")
    counts = git_out("diff", "--cached", "--numstat", "-z", "-M", "HEAD")
    if raw is None or counts is None:
        return False
    words = iter(word for word in raw.split("\0") if word)
    for meta in words:
        old_mode, new_mode, _, _, status = meta.lstrip(":").split(" ")
        path = next(words, "")
        if (
            status != "M"
            or old_mode != new_mode
            or new_mode in (GITLINK, SYMLINK)
            or is_test_path(path)
        ):
            return False
    lines = 0
    for record in counts.split("\0"):
        if "\t" not in record:
            continue
        added, removed, _ = record.split("\t", 2)
        if not (added.isdigit() and removed.isdigit()):
            return False
        lines += int(added) + int(removed)
    return lines <= SMALL_FIX_LINES


# How much of a message file the gate reads for its subject: the first line is all it
# needs, and the cut may split a character, so bytes that aren't UTF-8 are replaced.
MESSAGE_HEAD_BYTES = 4096


def file_subject(path):
    """The subject of the message file `-F path` names: its first non-blank line, as
    git's cleanup leaves it. None for `-`, stdin, which git reads only after the hook
    has run; for anything but a regular file, such as the pipe `-F <(...)` names,
    which a read would leave empty for git; and for a file that can't be read. git
    reads a relative path from where it started, which it passes hooks as GIT_PREFIX,
    relative to the top level the hook runs in."""
    if path == "-":
        return None
    message = Path(os.environ.get("GIT_PREFIX", "")) / path
    try:
        if not message.is_file():
            return None
        with message.open("rb") as file:
            head = file.read(MESSAGE_HEAD_BYTES).decode(errors="replace")
    except OSError:
        return None
    return next((line for line in head.splitlines() if line.strip()), "")


def is_fix_commit(args):
    """Whether a `git commit` with these arguments makes a fixup!, squash! or amend!
    commit, which build-state finish folds and wt-ship checks."""
    words, subject, message_file = iter(args), None, None
    for word in words:
        if word == "--":
            break
        if word.startswith(("--fixup", "--squash")):
            return True
        if word == "--message":
            message = next(words, "")
        elif word.startswith("--message="):
            message = word.removeprefix("--message=")
        elif word == "--file":
            message_file = next(words, "")
            continue
        elif word.startswith("--file="):
            message_file = word.removeprefix("--file=")
            continue
        elif word.startswith("--"):
            if word in COMMIT_LONG_VALUE:
                next(words, None)
            continue
        elif word.startswith("-") and len(word) > 1:
            # A cluster such as -am: the first letter that takes a value takes the
            # rest of the word, or the next word when it's last.
            message = None
            for j, letter in enumerate(word[1:], start=1):
                if letter in COMMIT_SHORT_VALUE or letter in COMMIT_SHORT_ATTACHED:
                    value = word[j + 1 :] or (
                        next(words, "") if letter in COMMIT_SHORT_VALUE else ""
                    )
                    if letter == "m":
                        message = value
                    elif letter == "F":
                        message_file = value
                    break
            if message is None:
                continue
        else:
            continue
        subject = message if subject is None else subject  # the first -m is the subject
    if subject is None and message_file is not None:
        subject = file_subject(message_file)  # git refuses -m with -F
    return subject is not None and subject.startswith(FIX_SUBJECT)


def process_args(pid):
    """(parent pid, argv) of a process: from /proc on Linux, else ps (whose args are
    joined by spaces, so a quoted word may split)."""
    proc = Path(f"/proc/{pid}")
    if proc.is_dir():
        try:
            argv = (proc / "cmdline").read_bytes().split(b"\0")
            stat = (proc / "stat").read_text()
            ppid = int(stat.rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()[1])
            return ppid, [arg.decode(errors="replace") for arg in argv if arg]
        except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
            return None
    run = subprocess.run(
        ["ps", "-o", "ppid=,args=", "-p", str(pid)],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=False,
    )
    fields = run.stdout.strip().split()
    if run.returncode != 0 or not fields:
        return None
    return int(fields[0]), fields[1:]


def running_commit_args():
    """The arguments of the `git commit` this hook runs under, found by walking up the
    process tree a few levels; None when there's none to be found."""
    pid = os.getppid()
    for _ in range(6):
        found = process_args(pid)
        if found is None:
            return None
        pid, argv = found
        if argv and Path(argv[0]).name == "git":
            rest, _ = git_subcommand(argv[1:])
            if rest[:1] == ["commit"]:
                return rest[1:]
        if pid <= 1:
            return None
    return None


def skips_hooks(args):
    """Whether a `git commit` with these arguments skips the pre-commit hook."""
    words = iter(args)
    for word in words:
        if word == "--":
            return False
        if word.startswith("--"):
            name = word.split("=", 1)[0]
            if len(name) >= NO_VERIFY_SHORTEST and NO_VERIFY.startswith(name):
                return True
            if word in COMMIT_LONG_VALUE:
                next(words, None)
        elif word.startswith("-") and len(word) > 1:
            for j, letter in enumerate(word[1:], start=1):
                if letter == "n":
                    return True
                if letter in COMMIT_SHORT_VALUE:
                    if j == len(word) - 1:
                        next(words, None)
                    break
                if letter in COMMIT_SHORT_ATTACHED:
                    break
    return False


def git_out(*args):
    """A git command's output, or None when it fails."""
    run = subprocess.run(["git", *args], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    return run.stdout.strip() if run.returncode == 0 else None


def hooks_dir(repo):
    hooks = git_out("-C", str(repo), "rev-parse", "--git-path", "hooks")
    return None if hooks is None else Path(repo, hooks)


def is_inert(line):
    """Whether a hook's line runs nothing: blank, or a comment."""
    return not line.strip() or line.lstrip().startswith("#")


def install(repo):
    """Put LINE first in the repo's pre-commit hook, replacing an older line; returns an
    error message or None."""
    hooks = hooks_dir(repo)
    if hooks is None:
        return f"{repo}: not a git repository"
    agent = hooks / HOOK_NAME
    if not (agent.is_file() and os.access(agent, os.X_OK)):
        return f"{repo}: no executable {agent}"
    pre = hooks / "pre-commit"
    exists = pre.exists()
    lines = (pre.read_text() if exists else "#!/bin/sh\n").splitlines()
    shebang = lines[0][2:].split() if lines and lines[0].startswith("#!") else []
    at = 1 if shebang else 0
    found = next(
        (i for i, line in enumerate(lines) if line.strip() in (LINE, *OLD_LINES)), None
    )
    # The line runs the whole hook again, so whatever runs above it would run twice.
    first = found is not None and all(map(is_inert, lines[at:found]))
    if first and lines[found].strip() == LINE:
        print(f"{pre}: already installed")
        return None
    if shebang and Path(shebang[0]).name == "env":
        shebang = shebang[1:]
    if shebang and Path(shebang[0]).name not in SHELLS:
        return f"{pre}: not a shell script, so add the agent check by hand"
    done = []
    if found is not None:
        if lines[found].strip() != LINE:
            done.append("upgraded")
        if not first:
            done.append(f"moved the line from line {found + 1} to the top")
        start = found
        if found > at and lines[found - 1].strip() in (COMMENT, *OLD_COMMENTS):
            start -= 1  # its comment goes with it
        del lines[start : found + 1]
    lines[at:at] = [COMMENT, LINE]
    pre.write_text("\n".join(lines) + "\n")
    if not exists:
        pre.chmod(0o755)
    print(f"{pre}: {', '.join(done) or 'installed'}")
    return None


def secret_named(name):
    """Whether a variable's name marks it a secret: one of its `_`-separated parts, in
    any case, is in SECRET_PARTS."""
    return not SECRET_PARTS.isdisjoint(name.upper().split("_"))


def check_env(env, base):
    """`env` for a check: CHECK_BASE set to `base` (None: unset), every secret-named
    variable and BASH_ENV left out, and CHECK_QUEUE_EXIT and AT_COMMIT left out, so
    nothing the check starts takes itself for a commit's."""
    gone = ("CHECK_BASE", QUEUE_EXIT_NAME, AT_COMMIT, "BASH_ENV")
    env = {k: v for k, v in env.items() if k not in gone and not secret_named(k)}
    if base:
        env["CHECK_BASE"] = base
    return env


@contextmanager
def on_one_core():
    """Pin this process, and so the check it starts, to the last core it may use, so
    a build the check starts outside the queue by mistake can't load the machine; its
    cores come back after."""
    cores = os.sched_getaffinity(0)
    try:
        os.sched_setaffinity(0, {max(cores)})
    except OSError as error:
        print(
            f"agent-commit-gate: can't pin to one core ({error.strerror}), so the run"
            " outside heavy's queue runs on any.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        yield
        return
    try:
        yield
    finally:
        with suppress(OSError):
            os.sched_setaffinity(0, cores)


def run_once(path, env):
    """The check's run, its output with stderr merged in, and how long it took."""
    started = time.monotonic()
    # `path` as given: Path() would drop a leading ./ and send it to a PATH lookup.
    run = subprocess.run(
        [path],
        stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
        stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
        check=False,
        env=env,
    )
    return run, time.monotonic() - started


def report(name, base, run, took, outside=False):
    """Print the check's pass line, or its output and the refusal; return its code."""
    code = run.returncode
    if code == 0:
        where = ", outside heavy's queue" if outside else ""
        given = f", given CHECK_BASE {base[:12]}" if base else ""
        print(f"{name}: passed ({took:.0f}s{where}{given})", file=sys.stderr)
    else:
        sys.stderr.write(run.stdout.decode(errors="replace"))
        print(f"COMMIT REFUSED: {name} failed (exit {code})", file=sys.stderr)
    return code


def run_outside_first(path, name, base, queue=True):
    """Run the check as run_quiet does, but first outside heavy's queue, on one core,
    with CHECK_QUEUE_EXIT set. That run's outcome is the check's, unless it exits with
    QUEUE_EXIT: then what it printed shows, as its reason, and it runs again in the
    queue, or without queue, returns QUEUE_EXIT for the caller to run it there."""
    env = check_env(os.environ, base) | {
        QUEUE_EXIT_NAME: str(QUEUE_EXIT),
        "CARGO_BUILD_JOBS": "1",
    }
    with on_one_core():
        run, took = run_once(path, env)
    if run.returncode != QUEUE_EXIT:
        return report(name, base, run, took, outside=True)
    sys.stderr.write(run.stdout.decode(errors="replace"))
    return run_quiet(path, name, base) if queue else QUEUE_EXIT


def run_quiet(path, name, base, queue=True):
    """Run the check at `path`, named `name` in what it prints, with CHECK_BASE set to
    `base` (None: unset, whatever this process inherited), and return its exit code.
    Its output shows only when it fails: agents filtered a noisy pass with greps, and
    the greps hid failed commits. With queue, it runs once it's its turn in heavy's
    queue, whose waiting lines print as they come."""
    with ExitStack() as stack:
        env, job = os.environ, None
        try:
            if queue:
                job = stack.enter_context(heavyqueue.turn(command=name, hint=False))
                job.pin()  # the check inherits the job's cores
                env = job.env
        except heavyqueue.QueueError as error:
            print(
                f"heavy: can't use its queue ({error}), so the check runs without it.",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
        run, took = run_once(path, check_env(env, base))
        if job is not None:
            job.exited(run.returncode)  # a passing check goes into heavy's history
    return report(name, base, run, took)


class Check(NamedTuple):
    """A check the gate may run, its bytes, and the record of the trees that passed it
    (`passrecord`)."""

    path: str
    name: str
    content: bytes
    record: Path | None
    passed: frozenset

    @classmethod
    def at(cls, path, name, record_name):
        record = passrecord.record_path(record_name)
        passed = frozenset(passrecord.recorded(record)) if record else frozenset()
        return cls(path, name, Path(path).read_bytes(), record, passed)

    def key(self, tree):
        """The record's line for `tree` passing this check as its bytes are now."""
        return passrecord.pass_key(tree, passrecord.fingerprint(self.content))

    def passed_tree(self, tree):
        return self.key(tree) in self.passed


def commit_check(path):
    """The repo's COMMIT_CHECK_NAME beside `path`, as a Check, when the line says this
    run is a commit's and the repo has one; None otherwise."""
    if os.environ.get("AGENT_CHECK_AT_COMMIT") != "1":
        return None
    found = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(path), COMMIT_CHECK_NAME)
    if not (os.path.isfile(found) and os.access(found, os.X_OK)):
        return None
    return Check.at(found, COMMIT_CHECK_NAME, passrecord.COMMIT_CHECK_RECORD)


def check_base(checks):
    """The nearest of HEAD's first BASE_SEARCH first-parent commits, HEAD included,
    whose tree passed one of `checks`; None when there's none."""
    log = git_out(
        "log",
        "--no-show-signature",
        "--first-parent",
        f"-n{BASE_SEARCH}",
        "--format=%H %T",
        "HEAD",
    )
    for line in (log or "").splitlines():
        # Only `<commit> <tree>`, whatever else a config adds to git log's output.
        words = line.split()
        if len(words) == 2 and any(check.passed_tree(words[1]) for check in checks):
            return words[0]
    return None


def staged():
    """What the index holds, entry by entry. Unlike write-tree, ls-files takes no lock,
    so another git command running at once can't make it fail."""
    return git_out("ls-files", "--stage", "-z")


def refuse_if_staged_changed(code, before, name):
    """The check's exit code `code`, or 1 when the check passed but what's staged
    differs from `before`: the check didn't see what would be committed."""
    if code == 0 and staged() != before:
        print(
            f"COMMIT REFUSED: what's staged changed while {name} ran (it restaged a"
            " file, or a git add ran meanwhile), so its pass doesn't cover what would"
            " be committed; run it again on what's staged now",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1
    return code


def no_unstaged_changes():
    """Whether tracked files in the work tree match the index."""
    return subprocess.run(["git", "diff", "--quiet"], check=False).returncode == 0


def run_check(path, queue=True):
    """Run the agent check at `path`, or at a commit the repo's COMMIT_CHECK_NAME in
    its place (commit_check), unless HEAD's tree is the tree being checked and passed
    already, as when an amend changes only the message or wt-ship rechecks a commit it
    didn't rebase. At a commit a pass of either check counts; otherwise only the agent
    check's. The key is the tree and the check's own content, on a record every
    worktree shares, one record per check. A pass counts only with no unstaged changes
    to tracked files, since some checks read the working tree rather than the index.
    The check gets CHECK_BASE, the nearest first-parent commit whose tree passed a
    check that counts. A check that passes but leaves what's staged changed refuses
    the commit. Without queue (--no-queue), a check that would wait in heavy's queue
    doesn't run there: --run exits with QUEUE_EXIT, recording nothing, for the caller to
    run it in a job of its own, as wt-ship does with a landing's checks."""
    hook = Path(path)
    if (busy := commitcheck.blocking_commits()) is not None:
        print(
            f"COMMIT REFUSED: wt-ship ({busy.holder}) is checking earlier commits in"
            " this worktree, which holds one of them until it ends, so a commit now"
            " would be lost when the tip comes back. Wait for it to end, then commit;"
            f" if no wt-ship is running there, delete {busy.path}.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1
    if not (hook.is_file() and os.access(hook, os.X_OK)):
        print(f"{path}: no executable agent check", file=sys.stderr)
        print(f"COMMIT REFUSED: no {HOOK_NAME} to run", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    full = Check.at(path, HOOK_NAME, passrecord.PASS_RECORD)
    at_commit = commit_check(path)
    counted = [check for check in (full, at_commit) if check is not None]
    before = staged()
    fix = (args := running_commit_args()) is not None and is_fix_commit(args)
    if fix and is_small_fix():
        # The quick check's base passed the full check, whose checks include its own.
        base = check_base([full])
        # Beside `path` as given, so a ./ path stays one.
        quick = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(path), QUICK_NAME)
        if os.path.isfile(quick) and os.access(quick, os.X_OK):
            # Fast static checks only, no tests: not worth a place in heavy's queue,
            # unless it asks for one.
            if names_queue_exit(Path(quick).read_bytes()):
                code = run_outside_first(quick, QUICK_NAME, base)
            else:
                code = run_quiet(quick, QUICK_NAME, base, queue=False)
            return refuse_if_staged_changed(code, before, QUICK_NAME)
        print(
            f"{HOOK_NAME}: skipped for a small fix commit; wt-ship runs it on each "
            "commit it lands with no pass on record",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 0
    tree = git_out("write-tree")
    if tree and tree == git_out("rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", "HEAD^{tree}"):
        for check in counted:
            if check.passed_tree(tree):
                print(
                    f"{check.name}: skipped, tree {tree[:12]} already passed it",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
                return 0
    check = at_commit or full
    base = check_base(counted)
    clean = no_unstaged_changes()
    if heavyqueue.inside_job():
        # The job holds the lane already, so a run outside the queue would be a
        # second one: wt-ship's job for a landing's checks.
        code = run_quiet(check.path, check.name, base)
    elif names_queue_exit(check.content):
        code = run_outside_first(check.path, check.name, base, queue=queue)
    elif queue:
        code = run_quiet(check.path, check.name, base)
    else:
        return QUEUE_EXIT  # it waits in the queue: the caller's to run there
    code = refuse_if_staged_changed(code, before, check.name)
    # Some checks read the work tree, so it must match the index from start to end.
    if code == 0 and clean and no_unstaged_changes() and tree and check.record:
        passrecord.remember(check.record, check.key(tree), check.name)
    return code


def main(argv=None):
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--install",
        nargs="+",
        metavar="REPO",
        help="add the agent check line to each repo's pre-commit hook",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--run",
        metavar="HOOK",
        help="run the agent check HOOK with CHECK_BASE, unless the tree checked is "
        "HEAD's and passed it",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--no-queue",
        action="store_true",
        help=f"with --run: exit {QUEUE_EXIT} instead of waiting in heavy's queue",
    )
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if args.run:
        try:
            return run_check(args.run, queue=not args.no_queue)
        except KeyboardInterrupt:
            print("COMMIT REFUSED: the agent check was stopped.", file=sys.stderr)
            return 130
    if args.install:
        errors = [e for e in map(install, args.install) if e]
        for error in errors:
            print(error, file=sys.stderr)
        return 1 if errors else 0
    event = json.load(sys.stdin)
    if event.get("tool_name") != "Bash":
        return 0
    command = event.get("tool_input", {}).get("command", "")
    cwd = Path(event.get("cwd") or ".")
    if any(
        skips_hooks(args) and has_agent_check(cwd / directory)
        for args, directory in commits(command)
    ):
        print(
            "Commit blocked: --no-verify (-n) skips the pre-commit hook, which runs "
            f"this repo's checks, {HOOK_NAME} included. Fix what they report and "
            "commit without it. An amend that changes only the message already skips "
            "the agent check, since its tree passed it.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 2
    return 0


def has_agent_check(directory):
    """Whether the repo at `directory` has an agent check, or can't be told: a scratch
    repo without one may skip its hooks."""
    hooks = hooks_dir(directory) if directory.is_dir() else None
    if hooks is None:
        return True
    agent = hooks / HOOK_NAME
    return agent.is_file() and os.access(agent, os.X_OK)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
